			<h1 class="ba-h1" id="article-title">PHP2Shell (CVE-2026-109618 &amp; CVE-2026-109619): Critical PHP RCE Flaws, Affected Versions, and How to Fix Them</h1>
			<div class="ba-article">
				
<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>[Update] October 23, 2026: Exploitation attempts began on October 17, four days before PHP released fixes. SOCRadar found</em> <em>PHP2Shell exploit tooling</em> <em>on an exposed attacker server. PHP releases new patches.</em></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><em>[Update] October 21, 2026, 14:00 UTC: PHP has released fixes for both vulnerabilities. See the subheading</em> <em><a href="#update1">“PHP Releases Patches for PHP2Shell.”</a></em></p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">PHP2Shell is the name researchers now use for two critical vulnerabilities in PHP, the server-side language behind WordPress, Drupal, Magento, Laravel, and many custom web applications. Tracked as CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619, both flaws reportedly allow <a href="https://socradar.io/glossary/remote-code-execution-rce/">Remote Code Execution (RCE)</a>, and the PHP security team rated each 9.2 on CVSS v4.0.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The flaws surfaced ahead of schedule. <strong>Their existence was leaked on October 20, 2026</strong>, two days before a coordinated disclosure, and the PHP security team responded that evening with an advance notice of an upcoming security release set for October 21, 2026, at 14:00 Coordinated Universal Time (UTC).</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" onerror="if(/\.webp/i.test(this.currentSrc||this.src)){this.srcset=this.srcset.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp/gi,'$1');this.src=this.src.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp$/i,'$1')}else{this.onerror=null;this.removeAttribute('srcset')}" width="2048" height="827" src="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/php2shell-upcoming-security-release-advance-notice.jpg.webp" alt="Advance notice of the upcoming security release for PHP2Shell vulnerabilities" class="wp-image-13565" srcset="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/php2shell-upcoming-security-release-advance-notice.jpg 2048w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/php2shell-upcoming-security-release-advance-notice-300x121.jpg 300w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/php2shell-upcoming-security-release-advance-notice-1024x414.jpg 1024w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/php2shell-upcoming-security-release-advance-notice-768x310.jpg 768w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/php2shell-upcoming-security-release-advance-notice-1536x620.jpg 1536w" sizes="(max-width: 2048px) 100vw, 2048px"><figcaption class="wp-element-caption has-text-align-center" style="text-align:center"><em><em> Advance notice of the upcoming security release for PHP2Shell vulnerabilities </em></em></figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://socradar.io/free-tools/php2shell-checker" data-type="link" data-id="https://socradar.io/free-tools/php2shell-checker" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Before you continue: quickly check whether your domains are exposed with SOCRadar’s free PHP2Shell Checker.</a></p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="php2shell-at-a-glance">PHP2Shell at a Glance</h2>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th><strong>Attribute</strong></th><th><strong>Details</strong></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CVE ID</td><td>CVE-2026-109618, CVE-2026-109619</td></tr><tr><td>CVSS</td><td>9.2 CVSS v4.0 for each (PHP security team)</td></tr><tr><td>Affected Products</td><td>PHP</td></tr><tr><td>Affected Versions</td><td>All PHP installations serving web requests on releases before the fixed versions, including end-of-life branches</td></tr><tr><td>Fixed In</td><td>8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36, and 8.2.35; source backports for 8.1, 8.0, and 7.4</td></tr><tr><td>Exploitation Status</td><td>In-the-wild exploitation reported by Amazon. SOCRadar also found PHP2Shell exploit tooling.</td></tr><tr><td>Patch Available</td><td>Yes, released October 21, 2026 at 14:00 UTC</td></tr><tr><td>Discovered By</td><td>Wiz Research (see question 8 for details)</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="php2shell-timeline">PHP2Shell Timeline</h2>



<style> .p2s-tl{--navy:#1B1B3C;--coral:#FF4562;--text:#25253A;--muted:#6C6C7E;--line:#E9EAF0;--bg:#F4F5F8;font-family:Inter,-apple-system,"Segoe UI",Roboto,sans-serif;color:var(--text);max-width:820px;margin:0 auto;line-height:1.6} .p2s-tl *{box-sizing:border-box} .p2s-tl ol{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0 0 0 28px;position:relative} .p2s-tl ol:before{content:"";position:absolute;left:7px;top:6px;bottom:6px;width:2px;background:var(--line)} .p2s-tl li{position:relative;margin:0 0 18px;padding:0} .p2s-tl li:before{content:"";position:absolute;left:-27px;top:20px;width:14px;height:14px;border-radius:50%;background:#fff;border:3px solid var(--navy)} .p2s-tl li.p2s-key:before{border-color:var(--coral);background:var(--coral)} .p2s-tl .p2s-card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:14px;padding:16px 20px;box-shadow:0 2px 8px rgba(27,27,60,.05)} .p2s-tl .p2s-date{font-family:"IBM Plex Mono",ui-monospace,monospace;font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);margin:0 0 4px} .p2s-tl .p2s-tag{display:inline-block;font-family:Inter,sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:600;letter-spacing:.04em;text-transform:uppercase;border-radius:999px;padding:2px 10px;margin-left:8px;vertical-align:1px} .p2s-tl .t-exploit{background:#FFE3E8;color:#C21D3B} .p2s-tl .t-socradar{background:var(--navy);color:#fff} .p2s-tl .t-patch{background:#E3F4EA;color:#1E7A45} .p2s-tl .t-disclosure{background:#ECECF4;color:var(--navy)} .p2s-tl .t-vendor{background:#FFF1DC;color:#8A5300} .p2s-tl h3{font-family:"Red Hat Display",Inter,sans-serif;font-size:18px;line-height:1.35;color:var(--navy);margin:0 0 6px} .p2s-tl .p2s-card p{margin:0;font-size:15px} .p2s-tl .p2s-card ul{margin:8px 0 0;padding-left:20px;font-size:15px} .p2s-tl .p2s-card li{margin:0 0 4px;padding:0} .p2s-tl .p2s-card li:before{display:none} .p2s-tl a{color:var(--coral);font-weight:600;text-decoration:none} .p2s-tl a:hover{text-decoration:underline} ＠media (max-width:600px){.p2s-tl .p2s-card{padding:14px 16px}.p2s-tl .p2s-tag{margin:4px 0 0;margin-left:0}} </style><div class="p2s-tl"><ol><li><div class="p2s-card"><p class="p2s-date">October 23, 2026 <span class="p2s-tag t-disclosure">Disclosure</span></p><h3>PHP warns of another unpatched session flaw</h3><p>PHP discloses CVE-2026-115560, a separate session-handling flaw that the October 21 releases do not fix. No exploitation has been reported, and the fix will ship in the next routine bugfix release. Setting <code>session.upload_progress.enabled=0</code> mitigates it. <a href="https://www.php.net/archive/2026.php#2026-10-23-1" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Read the advisory</a></p></div></li><li class="p2s-key"><div class="p2s-card"><p class="p2s-date">October 23, 2026 <span class="p2s-tag t-socradar">SOCRadar</span></p><h3>SOCRadar publishes the PHP2Shell Flash Report</h3><p>The report covers pre-patch exploitation, exposure data, the attacker toolkit, underground forum reaction, affected vendor products, IOCs, MITRE ATT&amp;CK mapping, and patching and hunting guidance. <a href="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/PHP2Shell-Flash-Report.pdf">Read the flash report</a></p></div></li><li><div class="p2s-card"><p class="p2s-date">October 23, 2026 <span class="p2s-tag t-disclosure">Status</span></p><h3>Neither CVE is in CISA’s KEV catalog yet</h3><p>As of October 23, CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619 are not listed in CISA’s Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog. On October 21, CISA said it had not observed exploitation.</p></div></li><li><div class="p2s-card"><p class="p2s-date">October 22–23, 2026 <span class="p2s-tag t-vendor">Vendors</span></p><h3>Network appliance vendors confirm affected products</h3><ul><li><strong>Palo Alto Networks:</strong> PAN-OS 12.2, 12.1, 11.2, 11.1 and 10.2 and all Panorama versions are affected, with no fixed release yet</li><li><strong>Cisco:</strong> Expressway and Expressway Select are rated Critical, with 15.5.21 as the fixed release; other products are under investigation</li><li><strong>Fortinet and Splunk:</strong> Several products are under investigation</li></ul></div></li><li class="p2s-key"><div class="p2s-card"><p class="p2s-date">October 22, 2026 <span class="p2s-tag t-socradar">SOCRadar</span></p><h3>PHP2Shell exploit tooling found on an exposed attacker server</h3><p>SOCRadar researchers found a lab-tested PHP2Shell exploit chain for PHP 8.4.26 under Apache mod_php, along with scripts that screened about 20 public websites in Europe, North America and Asia. None of the screened sites met the exploit’s preconditions. <a href="https://socradar.io/blog/php2shell-exploit-tooling-attacker-server/">Read the findings</a></p></div></li><li><div class="p2s-card"><p class="p2s-date">October 22, 2026 <span class="p2s-tag t-exploit">Exploitation</span></p><h3>Exploitation described as broad</h3><p>Exploitation is now described as broad in the wild, though it remains unclear how many attacks succeed. October 22 was also the original coordinated disclosure date.</p></div></li><li class="p2s-key"><div class="p2s-card"><p class="p2s-date">October 21, 2026, 14:00 <span class="p2s-tag t-patch">Patch</span></p><h3>PHP releases fixes</h3><p>PHP releases 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36 and 8.2.35. PHP 8.1 and older get no upstream release. Honeypots observe exploitation of both CVEs the same day.</p></div></li><li><div class="p2s-card"><p class="p2s-date">October 20, 2026 <span class="p2s-tag t-disclosure">Disclosure</span></p><h3>Details leak ahead of coordinated disclosure</h3><p>Details of the two critical PHP RCE flaws leak, including the CVE IDs and mitigation advice given to some critical infrastructure providers. The PHP security team publishes an advance notice of the security release, and CERT-EU publishes Security Advisory 2026-018.</p></div></li><li class="p2s-key"><div class="p2s-card"><p class="p2s-date">October 17, 2026 <span class="p2s-tag t-exploit">Exploitation</span></p><h3>First exploitation attempts observed</h3><p>The first attempts come from an IP address tied to a commercial anonymization network, four days before PHP releases fixes.</p></div></li></ol></div>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="update1">PHP Releases Patches for PHP2Shell</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">On October 21, 2026, PHP released 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36, and 8.2.35 to fix both PHP2Shell vulnerabilities. Its <a href="https://www.php.net/index.php#2026-10-21-5" target="_blank" rel="noopener">security advisory</a> confirms that the flaws can allow unauthenticated remote code execution and urges all PHP users to update immediately.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th>CVE ID</th><th>Advisory</th><th>What Goes Wrong</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><a href="https://socradar.io/free-tools/cve-radar/CVE-2026-109618" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CVE-2026-109618</a></td><td>GHSA-w8hw-w9fx-j79w</td><td>RCE via out-of-memory corruption: when memory runs out mid-request, PHP can be interrupted while internal request data is only partly set up, and cleanup may then treat that broken data as valid</td></tr><tr><td><a href="https://socradar.io/free-tools/cve-radar/CVE-2026-109619" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CVE-2026-109619</a></td><td>GHSA-6wmf-4rv5-p3rp</td><td>RCE via stale global state: internal state tied to a request can outlive its freed memory, and a later request on the same worker may use it before it is reset</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">PHP rates the risk highest for Apache with <strong>mod_php</strong>, where it describes a demonstrated exploit that lets an unauthenticated request trigger the attack before any application code runs. <strong>PHP-FPM</strong>, <strong>php-cgi</strong>, <strong>FrankenPHP</strong>, and <strong>LiteSpeed</strong> deployments are also at risk.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="php-warns-of-another-unpatched-session-flaw">PHP Warns of Another Unpatched Session Flaw</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">On October 23, 2026, PHP <a href="https://www.php.net/archive/2026.php#2026-10-23-1">disclosed CVE-2026-115560</a>, a separate session-handling flaw that the October 21 releases do not fix. It affects only applications that switch session.serialize_handler from php_binary or php_serialize to php with ini_set() before calling session_start(), while session upload progress is enabled. PHP considers this setup unlikely in practice. No exploitation has been reported, and no severity score has been published yet. The fix will ship in PHP’s next routine bugfix release.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Recommended action:</strong> Set session.upload_progress.enabled=0, the same mitigation PHP issued for CVE-2026-109619. Organizations that have already applied it need to do nothing more. Upgrading to the October 21 releases is still required to fix CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="1-what-is-php2shell">1. What Is PHP2Shell?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">PHP2Shell is the nickname for two critical PHP vulnerabilities, CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619, that researchers say let a remote attacker run code on a server running PHP. The name points to the expected result, a shell on the target.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Because the fix ships in PHP itself, protection for WordPress, Drupal, Magento, and other PHP applications depends on updating the server’s PHP version. Updating the application alone does not close the gap.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="2-how-did-php2shell-become-public-before-the-patch">2. How Did PHP2Shell Become Public Before the Patch?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">PHP2Shell became public through a leak. On October 20, 2026, details surfaced claiming that two PHP RCE flaws, rated critical and affecting every version since PHP 5, would be disclosed on Thursday, October 22, 2026. watchTowr then confirmed on X that the flaws were real and under embargo, and urged users to apply patches once available.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" onerror="if(/\.webp/i.test(this.currentSrc||this.src)){this.srcset=this.srcset.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp/gi,'$1');this.src=this.src.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp$/i,'$1')}else{this.onerror=null;this.removeAttribute('srcset')}" width="1064" height="414" src="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/watchtowr-confirms-php2shell-vulnerabilities-leak-x.jpg.webp" alt="WatchTowr’s post on X, confirming the leak of PHP2Shell vulnerabilities" class="wp-image-13566" srcset="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/watchtowr-confirms-php2shell-vulnerabilities-leak-x.jpg 1064w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/watchtowr-confirms-php2shell-vulnerabilities-leak-x-300x117.jpg 300w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/watchtowr-confirms-php2shell-vulnerabilities-leak-x-1024x398.jpg 1024w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/watchtowr-confirms-php2shell-vulnerabilities-leak-x-768x299.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1064px) 100vw, 1064px"><figcaption class="wp-element-caption has-text-align-center" style="text-align:center"><em><em> WatchTowr’s post on </em><em><a href="https://x.com/watchtowrcyber/status/2112611507749982605">X</a></em><em>, confirming the leak of PHP2Shell vulnerabilities</em></em></figcaption></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="3-how-severe-are-cve-2026-109618-and-cve-2026-109619">3. How Severe Are CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The PHP security team rates both flaws <strong>9.2 on CVSS v4.0</strong>, which is critical severity. PHP’s advisory traces CVE-2026-109618 to out-of-memory handling and CVE-2026-109619 to stale global state, and confirms that both can be exploited without authentication.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://cert.europa.eu/publications/security-advisories/2026-018/">CERT-EU</a>, the <a href="/glossary/cybersecurity/">cybersecurity</a> service for European Union institutions, says the 9.2 scores point to possible unauthenticated RCE.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="4-which-php-versions-are-affected-by-php2shell">4. Which PHP Versions Are Affected by PHP2Shell?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">PHP released fixes for both vulnerabilities on October 21, 2026. Affected releases in supported branches are PHP 8.5 before 8.5.12, PHP 8.4 before 8.4.27, PHP 8.3 before 8.3.36, and PHP 8.2 before 8.2.35. Older, end-of-life branches are also affected.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The table below lists the fixed releases and available source backports:</p>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Fixed Version</th><th>Support Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>8.5</td><td>8.5.12</td><td>Active support until December 31, 2027</td></tr><tr><td>8.4</td><td>8.4.27</td><td>Active support until December 31, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>8.3</td><td>8.3.36</td><td>Security fixes only until December 31, 2027</td></tr><tr><td>8.2</td><td>8.2.35</td><td>Security fixes only until December 31, 2026</td></tr><tr><td>8.1, 8.0, 7.4</td><td>Minimal source backports only</td><td>End of life</td></tr><tr><td>7.3 and older</td><td>No fix from PHP</td><td>End of life</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="5-will-end-of-life-php-versions-get-a-php2shell-fix">5. Will End-of-Life PHP Versions Get a PHP2Shell Fix?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Not through regular releases. PHP 8.1 and older no longer receive official updates, but given the severity, PHP’s security team published minimal source backports for PHP 7.4, 8.0, and 8.1 for anyone building from source. Some distributions and long-term support (LTS) vendors also ship backported fixes, but upgrading to a supported branch remains the only lasting remedy.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Shodan lists around 884,000 internet-facing hosts running PHP 8.1 or older. An additional 740,000 hosts run PHP without specifying a version number.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img decoding="async" onerror="if(/\.webp/i.test(this.currentSrc||this.src)){this.srcset=this.srcset.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp/gi,'$1');this.src=this.src.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp$/i,'$1')}else{this.onerror=null;this.removeAttribute('srcset')}" width="1416" height="1214" src="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/shodan-php-hosts-version-analysis-source.jpg.webp" alt="Shodan results for PHP hosts by version (Source)" class="wp-image-13567" srcset="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/shodan-php-hosts-version-analysis-source.jpg 1416w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/shodan-php-hosts-version-analysis-source-300x257.jpg 300w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/shodan-php-hosts-version-analysis-source-1024x878.jpg 1024w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/shodan-php-hosts-version-analysis-source-768x658.jpg 768w" sizes="(max-width: 1416px) 100vw, 1416px"><figcaption class="wp-element-caption has-text-align-center" style="text-align:center"><em><em> Shodan results for PHP hosts by version (</em><em><a href="https://mallory.ai/blog/php-rce-rapid-response">Source</a></em><em>) </em></em></figcaption></figure>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="6-how-to-check-if-your-site-is-vulnerable-to-php2shell">6. How to Check If Your Site Is Vulnerable to PHP2Shell?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Use SOCRadar’s free PHP2Shell Checker to check the PHP version exposed by your domain against the fixed releases. The tool uses passive signals such as response headers and flags end-of-life branches. It requires no signup or agent and does not attempt exploitation.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large"><a href="https://socradar.io/free-tools/php2shell-checker" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" onerror="if(/\.webp/i.test(this.currentSrc||this.src)){this.srcset=this.srcset.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp/gi,'$1');this.src=this.src.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp$/i,'$1')}else{this.onerror=null;this.removeAttribute('srcset')}" width="1024" height="565" src="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/p2pshell-checker-1024x565.png.webp" alt="SOCRadar's free PHP2Shell Checker" class="wp-image-13601" srcset="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/p2pshell-checker-1024x565.png 1024w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/p2pshell-checker-300x165.png 300w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/p2pshell-checker-768x423.png 768w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/p2pshell-checker-1536x847.png 1536w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/p2pshell-checker-2048x1129.png 2048w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"></a><figcaption class="wp-element-caption"><em><a href="https://socradar.io/free-tools/php2shell-checker" data-type="link" data-id="https://socradar.io/free-tools/php2shell-checker" target="_blank" rel="noopener">SOCRadar’s free PHP2Shell Checker</a></em></figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">An inconclusive result does not mean your site is safe. If the version is hidden, confirm the PHP runtime through your hosting panel or server administrator.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">On the server, <code>php -v</code> checks the command-line installation, which may differ from the PHP runtime serving web requests. Verify the web runtime separately and consult your distribution’s security advisory, since vendor packages may include backported fixes without changing the upstream version number.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>Vendor Products Affected by PHP2Shell</strong></h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">PHP2Shell also reaches network appliances and products that bundle PHP. Vendor advisories as of October 22 and 23 report the following:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Palo Alto Networks:</strong> PAN-OS 12.2, 12.1, 11.2, 11.1 and 10.2 and all Panorama versions are affected, with no fixed release yet. Interim steps are Threat Prevention signatures (Threat IDs 543004 and 97622 to 97625), moving management traffic off the management port and restricting management access. Palo Alto Networks is not aware of malicious exploitation.</li>



<li><strong>Cisco:</strong> Expressway and Expressway Select (CSCwx37062) are rated Critical, with 15.5.21 listed as the fixed release and no workaround. Snort rules 67359 and 67360 are available. Ultra-Reliable Wireless Backhaul (CSCwx37073) is also affected. ISE Portal Builder, Catalyst Center and WAE are under investigation.</li>



<li><strong>Fortinet:</strong> FortiADC Manager, FortiClient Cloud Services, FortiDDoS and FortiDDoS-F are under investigation.</li>



<li><strong>Splunk:</strong> No affected products are confirmed so far. OnCall, AppDynamics SaaS and Smartlook are under investigation.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="7-is-php2shell-being-exploited-in-the-wild">7. Is PHP2Shell Being Exploited in the Wild?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Yes</strong>. Exploitation attempts started before the patches were released. Amazon threat intelligence <a href="https://www.bleepingcomputer.com/news/security/amazon-says-php2shell-exploitation-began-4-days-before-patches/">reported</a> that the first attempts began on Saturday, October 17, four days before PHP shipped fixes. The attempt came from an IP address associated with a commercial anonymization network and targeted a honeypot, which suggests a <a href="/glossary/threat-actors/">threat actor</a> was behind it. How that actor got exploit information before disclosure is still unclear.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Multiple PoC exploits were deployed from VPN IP addresses within hours of disclosure. Payloads and anonymization infrastructure varied, which points to multiple actors. Amazon now describes the flaws as broadly exploited in the wild, but it is unclear how many attacks succeed. Its guidance for defenders is direct: do not assume the public disclosure date is the earliest date of exploitation.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Several other security firms also detected unsuccessful exploitation attempts before public disclosure. Honeypot data recorded exploitation of both CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619 on October 21. Other threat intelligence reporting describes observed activity as limited to scanning, fingerprinting and non-working exploits, with no known compromises. That reporting also warns that working PoCs or successful exploitation are only a matter of time.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Some PoC exploits were published before the patches, and PHP said none of them were functional. PHP core developer Gina Peter Banyard said applying the official patches is the only permanent defense. The UK National Cyber Security Centre (NCSC) has told UK organizations to update PHP servers immediately or take them offline until they can be patched.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">On October 21, CISA said it had not observed exploitation. As of October 23, neither CVE is in the KEV catalog <strong>yet</strong>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The exposure is large. PHP powers up to 80% of websites. More than 5.1 million PHP hosts are tracked as exposed online, and about 3.8 million of them have at least one port open to attack.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What SOCRadar Sees on the Dark Web</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Concern about PHP2Shell has also reached the <a href="https://socradar.io/blog/top-10-deep-web-and-dark-web-forums/">Dark Web</a>. Some <a href="/blog/top-5-underground-hacker-forums-that-are-accessible-via-your-web-browsers-such-as-google-chrome-firefox-and-internet-explorer/">underground forums</a>, such as <strong>BreachForums</strong> and <strong>Dread</strong>, have taken their sites offline as a precaution.</p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" onerror="if(/\.webp/i.test(this.currentSrc||this.src)){this.srcset=this.srcset.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp/gi,'$1');this.src=this.src.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp$/i,'$1')}else{this.onerror=null;this.removeAttribute('srcset')}" width="1024" height="462" src="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bf-offline-php2shell-october-1024x462.png.webp" alt="BreachForums Telegram channel message saying the forum will be offline until PHP is patched globally." class="wp-image-13589" srcset="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bf-offline-php2shell-october-1024x462.png 1024w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bf-offline-php2shell-october-300x135.png 300w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bf-offline-php2shell-october-768x347.png 768w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bf-offline-php2shell-october-1536x694.png 1536w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/bf-offline-php2shell-october.png 1674w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"><figcaption class="wp-element-caption"><em>BreachForums Telegram channel message saying the forum will be offline until PHP is patched globally.</em></figcaption></figure>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" onerror="if(/\.webp/i.test(this.currentSrc||this.src)){this.srcset=this.srcset.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp/gi,'$1');this.src=this.src.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp$/i,'$1')}else{this.onerror=null;this.removeAttribute('srcset')}" width="1024" height="122" src="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/dread-disabled-php-1-1024x122.png.webp" alt="Dread maintenance notice saying access is disabled after the disclosure of CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619." class="wp-image-13590" srcset="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/dread-disabled-php-1-1024x122.png 1024w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/dread-disabled-php-1-300x36.png 300w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/dread-disabled-php-1-768x92.png 768w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/dread-disabled-php-1-1536x183.png 1536w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/dread-disabled-php-1.png 1668w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"><figcaption class="wp-element-caption"><em>Dread maintenance notice saying access is disabled after the disclosure of CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619.</em></figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Hacker forums also show interest in a working exploit. In one PHP2Shell thread, a user asked others to share an exploit, noting the one they found did not work on all versions, and another said they were eager to start compromising servers. </p>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-large"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" onerror="if(/\.webp/i.test(this.currentSrc||this.src)){this.srcset=this.srcset.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp/gi,'$1');this.src=this.src.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp$/i,'$1')}else{this.onerror=null;this.removeAttribute('srcset')}" width="1024" height="655" src="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/php2shell-threat-actors-post-for-exploits-1024x655.png.webp" alt="Hacker forum thread requesting a PHP2Shell exploit" class="wp-image-13576" srcset="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/php2shell-threat-actors-post-for-exploits-1024x655.png 1024w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/php2shell-threat-actors-post-for-exploits-300x192.png 300w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/php2shell-threat-actors-post-for-exploits-768x491.png 768w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/php2shell-threat-actors-post-for-exploits.png 1287w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1024px) 100vw, 1024px"><figcaption class="wp-element-caption"><em>Hacker forum thread requesting a PHP2Shell exploit</em></figcaption></figure>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>PHP2Shell Exploit Tooling Found on an Exposed Attacker Server</strong></h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">SOCRadar researchers found <a href="https://socradar.io/blog/php2shell-exploit-tooling-attacker-server/">PHP2Shell exploit tooling</a> on an attacker-controlled server. The server’s working directory had an open, unauthenticated directory listing, and all files were collected passively.</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>aslr_exploit.py:</strong> A one-click exploit for PHP 8.4.26 under Apache mod_php, the last vulnerable 8.4 release. It recycles the PHP worker, leaks memory addresses through the session cookie to defeat ASLR, and abuses session upload progress handling to run a shell command. It defaults to a local target, which suggests testing against containers the operator controls</li>



<li><strong>Preconditions:</strong> Visible PHP errors, session upload progress enabled, session strict mode disabled, a very low memory limit and a single Apache worker</li>



<li><strong>Limitation:</strong> The payload only runs a very short command, so a real attack would need a second-stage script</li>



<li><strong>Screening scripts:</strong> php2shell_fp.py checked about 20 sites for PHP versions and session echo. deep_probe.py examined 11 sites for displayed errors, phpinfo pages, file inclusion and session injection. wp_attack.py enumerated users, plugins and XML-RPC methods on five WordPress sites. All traffic went through a rotating proxy pool on the same server</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Six of the 11 probed sites ran PHP 8.4 releases older than the fix, four of them on 8.4.26. None met the exploit’s preconditions. SOCRadar assesses with moderate confidence that the tooling targets CVE-2026-109618. There was no evidence of shell access on any screened site as of October 22. The operator is unattributed, and the motivation appears financial, given other files pointing to activity against financial and Web3 targets.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The hardened settings that blocked this chain will not stop every variant. Updating PHP remains the fix.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="8-is-there-a-public-php2shell-exploit-or-proof-of-concept-poc">8. Is There a Public PHP2Shell Exploit or Proof-of-Concept (PoC)?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Researchers have demonstrated exploitation of both vulnerabilities. Wiz published its research alongside PHP’s patches, and Datadog independently reproduced the flaws. PHP’s advisory also describes demonstrated exploitation against Apache with mod_php. These demonstrations should be distinguished from unverified exploit code circulating online..</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="9-how-did-researchers-discover-and-exploit-php2shell">9. How Did Researchers Discover and Exploit PHP2Shell?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><a href="https://www.wiz.io/blog/php2shell-php-rce" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wiz</a> researchers published their official write-up alongside PHP’s patches. The team says frontier AI models helped find the bugs and chain them into working exploits, and compares PHP2Shell’s potential reach to <strong>Heartbleed</strong> and <strong>Log4Shell</strong>. Here’s a quick look at the write-up:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Root cause:</strong> There are four bugs behind the two CVEs, which stem from how PHP handles fatal out-of-memory errors mid-request, which can leave stale internal state for a later request to reuse. PHP’s patch fixes the issue at its source, covering other variants too.</li>



<li><strong>Demonstration:</strong> The team chained the bugs into one exploit that ran code without authentication on out-of-the-box WordPress, Drupal, and Joomla deployments. Separately, it showed attackers can read PHP worker memory holding data such as passwords and API keys.</li>



<li><strong>Hunting signals:</strong> The write-up lists signs to watch for, such as unusual multipart requests followed by memory-limit errors or repeated 500 responses, and oversized or malformed <strong>PHPSESSID</strong> cookies.</li>



<li><strong>Disclosure timeline:</strong> The first bug was found on August 19, 2026, reported to PHP on August 24, 2026, and acknowledged by PHP on September 21, 2026.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="10-how-fast-could-attackers-exploit-php2shell-after-the-patch">10. How Fast Could Attackers Exploit PHP2Shell After the Patch?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Attackers could move within minutes, because published fixes show where the bugs are.When <a href="https://socradar.io/free-tools/cve-radar/CVE-2024-4577" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CVE-2024-4577</a>, a flaw in PHP’s Common Gateway Interface (CGI) mode on Windows, became public in June 2024, Akamai saw exploit attempts against its honeypots within 24 hours.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="11-php2shell-workarounds-what-helps-and-what-doesnt">11. PHP2Shell Workarounds: What Helps and What Doesn’t</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">No complete workaround exists, and PHP calls updating the only safe choice. For servers that cannot be updated immediately, PHP’s advisory lists configuration measures that reduce exposure to specific attack paths without making an installation safe:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>CVE-2026-109618:</strong> Set <strong>memory_limit=-1</strong> in a way applications cannot override, lower <strong>post_max_size</strong> (8M without file uploads, 32M with them), enforce request size and count limits at the web server or proxy, and use container or cgroup limits instead of <strong>RLIMIT_AS</strong> or <strong>RLIMIT_DATA</strong>.</li>



<li><strong>CVE-2026-109619:</strong> Set <strong>session.upload_progress.enabled=0</strong> in <strong>php.ini</strong> or server configuration and restart every worker, set <strong>fatal_error_backtraces=0</strong> on PHP 8.5, remove invalid per-request settings that emit diagnostics, and fix attacker-reachable fatal errors and timeouts.</li>
</ul>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">CERT-EU also advises considering isolating internet-facing applications from public networks until a fix is applied.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Disabling <code>session.upload_progress.enabled</code> alone does not fully protect against PHP2Shell. These configuration changes reduce exposure to specific attack paths; they do not replace patching.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A <a href="https://socradar.io/glossary/web-application-firewall-waf/">web application firewall</a> can filter some malicious requests before they reach PHP, but protection depends on rule coverage and whether traffic can bypass the WAF. Treat it as a temporary measure.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="12-how-can-security-teams-detect-php2shell-exploitation">12. How Can Security Teams Detect PHP2Shell Exploitation?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Monitor both request-processing anomalies and post-exploitation behavior. Look for unusual form submissions, memory-exhaustion errors during request initialization, repeated server errors, and malformed PHPSESSID cookies. Correlate these signals with process activity, since individual errors do not establish exploitation.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Alert when <strong>php-fpm</strong>, <strong>php-cgi</strong>, <strong>httpd</strong>, or <strong>apache2</strong> unexpectedly launch shells, script interpreters, or download tools such as <strong>curl</strong> and <strong>wget</strong>.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">If an alert fires, isolate the server, preserve web server and PHP logs, and hunt for web shells, new files in the web root, new cron jobs or scheduled tasks, and unexpected accounts.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading"><strong>PHP2Shell Indicators of Compromise</strong></h3>



<figure class="wp-block-table"><table class="has-fixed-layout"><tbody><tr><td><strong>Indicator</strong></td><td><strong>Type</strong></td><td><strong>Context</strong></td></tr><tr><td>206.188.197.105</td><td>IPv4</td><td>Attacker staging and operations server</td></tr><tr><td>Port 9999</td><td>Port</td><td>Exposed operator working directory (HTTP directory listing)</td></tr><tr><td>Port 8899</td><td>Port</td><td>Rotating proxy pool endpoint</td></tr><tr><td>Port 8888</td><td>Port</td><td>Out-of-band interaction listener</td></tr><tr><td>MARKER_[8 hex characters].txt</td><td>Upload filename</td><td>Session injection screening probe</td></tr><tr><td>fprint, dp</td><td>Session ID prefixes</td><td>Upload-progress screening probes</td></tr></tbody></table></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph"><strong>Detection additions</strong></p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li>Block 206.188.197.105 at the perimeter and search web and proxy logs for past connections</li>



<li>Hunt for multipart POSTs containing PHP_SESSION_UPLOAD_PROGRESS from unfamiliar sources, especially uploads named MARKER_ followed by eight hex characters</li>



<li>Set expose_php=Off to stop version disclosure in response headers</li>



<li>Treat activity from before October 21 as in scope when reviewing logs, since exploitation attempts began on October 17</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="13-how-can-socradar-help-track-php2shell-exposure">13. How Can SOCRadar Help Track PHP2Shell Exposure?</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">With fixes available and exploitation reported, organizations need to identify exposed PHP installations, prioritize patching, and monitor for signs of compromise.</p>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">SOCRadar can support that process through:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong><a href="https://socradar.io/products/attack-surface-management/?utm_campaign=blogpage&amp;utm_source=website&amp;utm_medium=blog&amp;utm_term=attacksurfacemanagement&amp;utm_content=blogasm">Attack Surface Management</a>:</strong> Helps identify internet-facing assets running PHP and prioritize exposed systems, including those on end-of-life branches, for patching or isolation.</li>



<li><strong><a href="https://socradar.io/products/cyber-threat-intelligence/vulnerability-intelligence/">Vulnerability Intelligence</a></strong>: Tracks severity, exploitation developments, KEV status, and affected and fixed versions for CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619.</li>



<li><strong><a href="https://socradar.io/free-tools/php2shell-checker" target="_blank" rel="noopener">PHP2Shell Checker</a></strong>: Provides a free external version check to help identify domains that need further verification or patching.</li>
</ul>



<figure class="wp-block-image aligncenter size-full"><img loading="lazy" decoding="async" onerror="if(/\.webp/i.test(this.currentSrc||this.src)){this.srcset=this.srcset.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp/gi,'$1');this.src=this.src.replace(/(\.(?:png|jpe?g))\.webp$/i,'$1')}else{this.onerror=null;this.removeAttribute('srcset')}" width="1707" height="868" src="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/socradar-vulnerability-intelligence-cyber-threat-module-1.jpg.webp" alt="SOCRadar's Vulnerability Intelligence, Cyber Threat Intelligence module" class="wp-image-13568" srcset="https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/socradar-vulnerability-intelligence-cyber-threat-module-1.jpg 1707w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/socradar-vulnerability-intelligence-cyber-threat-module-1-300x153.jpg 300w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/socradar-vulnerability-intelligence-cyber-threat-module-1-1024x521.jpg 1024w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/socradar-vulnerability-intelligence-cyber-threat-module-1-768x391.jpg 768w, https://socradar.io/wp-content/uploads/2026/10/socradar-vulnerability-intelligence-cyber-threat-module-1-1536x781.jpg 1536w" sizes="auto, (max-width: 1707px) 100vw, 1707px"><figcaption class="wp-element-caption has-text-align-center" style="text-align:center"><em><em> SOCRadar’s Vulnerability Intelligence, Cyber Threat Intelligence module</em></em></figcaption></figure>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Combining external asset visibility with vulnerability intelligence can help security teams find exposed PHP servers quickly while patching and compromise assessment proceed.</p>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="14-how-to-patch-php2shell">14. How to Patch PHP2Shell</h2>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Update the PHP runtime serving your application to a fixed upstream release or a verified vendor-patched package. Prioritize internet-facing systems, then update internal installations:</p>



<ul class="wp-block-list">
<li><strong>Inventory every install:</strong> Include PHP bundled in vendor appliances, Windows software, and hosting control panels such as cPanel and Plesk.</li>



<li><strong>Update now:</strong> Move to PHP 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36, or 8.2.35, internet-facing systems first, then restart the PHP FastCGI Process Manager (PHP-FPM) or web server so the new build loads.</li>



<li><strong>Check backports:</strong> Consult your distribution or hosting provider’s advisory and install the package containing the fix. Version numbers alone may not identify backported patches.</li>



<li><strong>Rebuild containers:</strong> Update the PHP base image, rebuild and redeploy affected containers, and replace workers still running the old runtime.</li>



<li><strong>Restrict unpatched systems:</strong> Remove public access or restrict access through a VPN or allowlist until patched. WAF rules provide supplementary protection.</li>



<li><strong>Verify and monitor:</strong> Confirm the web-serving runtime uses a fixed release or patched vendor package. Review logs for earlier compromise and monitor PHP workers for unexpected child processes.</li>
</ul>



<h2 class="wp-block-heading" id="frequently-asked-questions-about-php2shell">Frequently Asked Questions About PHP2Shell</h2>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">What Is PHP2Shell?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">PHP2Shell refers to CVE-2026-109618 and CVE-2026-109619, two critical vulnerabilities in PHP that can allow unauthenticated remote code execution. Each has a CVSS v4.0 score of 9.2.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Which PHP Versions Fix PHP2Shell?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">PHP 8.5.12, 8.4.27, 8.3.36, and 8.2.35 contain the fixes. Vendor packages may also include backported patches, so check your provider’s security advisory.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Is WordPress Affected by PHP2Shell?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yes, WordPress sites can be affected when their underlying PHP runtime is vulnerable. Updating WordPress or its plugins alone does not fix PHP; your hosting provider or administrator must update the runtime.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Does Disabling session.upload_progress Fix PHP2Shell?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">No. Disabling <code>session.upload_progress.enabled</code> may reduce exposure to a specific attack path, but it does not fully address both vulnerabilities. Patching remains necessary.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Does a WAF Protect Against PHP2Shell?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">A <a href="/glossary/web-application-firewall-waf/">WAF</a> may block some exploitation attempts, but it cannot guarantee protection against every variant or traffic that bypasses it. Use it as a temporary supporting control while patching.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Is PHP2Shell Being Exploited?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Yes. Security vendors have reported exploitation activity. Distinguish confirmed compromise from scanning and attempted exploitation when assessing individual reports.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">How Do I Check Whether My Site Is Vulnerable?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Start with SOCRadar’s PHP2Shell Checker, then verify the PHP runtime serving your website. An inconclusive external result does not establish that the site is safe.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Who Discovered PHP2Shell?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Wiz Research reported discovering the vulnerabilities and published its technical analysis alongside PHP’s patches. Its research describes using AI models to help identify bugs and develop demonstrations.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">When Did PHP2Shell Exploitation Start?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">The first exploitation attempts were observed on October 17, 2026, four days before PHP released fixes on October 21. Several security firms also detected unsuccessful attempts before public disclosure. Log reviews should cover the period before the patch date.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Is PHP2Shell in CISA’s KEV Catalog?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">No. As of October 23, 2026, neither CVE-2026-109618 nor CVE-2026-109619 is listed in CISA’s Known Exploited Vulnerabilities catalog. On October 21, CISA said it had not observed exploitation.</p>



<h3 class="wp-block-heading">Are Firewalls and Network Appliances Affected by PHP2Shell?</h3>



<p class="wp-block-paragraph">Some are. Palo Alto Networks PAN-OS and Panorama are affected, with no fixed release yet. Cisco Expressway is rated Critical. Other Cisco, Fortinet and Splunk products are under investigation. Check each vendor’s advisory, since a product missing from a list is not confirmed safe.</p>



